{"gid":"2026_02_MIA_SF","week":2,"away":"MIA","home":"SF","away_col":"#2CD3C6","home_col":"#E8506A","spread":10.5,"total":46.5,"fav":"SF","hpts":28,"apts":18,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 20 \u00b7 16:25","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Script leans SF.</strong> As a 10-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Christian McCaffrey</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile MIA throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Jalen Tolbert</strong>.</p><p>The <strong>SF</strong> host the <strong>MIA</strong> as favorites of <strong>10.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>46.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>SF 28\u201318</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>47% of its snaps with SF in front</strong>, <strong>39% within one score</strong>, and <strong>14% with SF chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>SF should control</strong>. A favorite of 10 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>SF</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>MIA</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>MIA</strong> to run more total plays than SF \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for MIA's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Efficiency edge to SF \u2014 a 47% success-rate offense against a MIA defense that allows 46% \u2014 points should come.</p><p><strong>George Kittle</strong> has room to work \u2014 MIA allow <strong>42 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p><strong>Jalen Tolbert</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 SF hold WR1s to just 51 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 SF are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.31 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>In the red zone MIA throw it (55% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Jalen Tolbert</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>Fade the MIA run \u2014 as 10-point dogs projected to chase, <strong>De'Von Achane</strong>'s carries get squeezed, though his receiving work ticks up in catch-up mode.</p><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>SF \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>SF are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 10-point favorite projected to lead for 47% of snaps, <strong>SF</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Christian McCaffrey</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>17 carries for 76 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as SF salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Mike Evans</strong> (7 targets, 59 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when SF lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>George Kittle</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 64 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>MIA \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>MIA are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 MIA project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>De'Von Achane</strong>'s rushing projection (16 carries, 87 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Jalen Tolbert</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, MIA funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>4 targets for 27 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 32 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 10-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 t